  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Since the initial momentum of the system is zero. To keep the final momentum zero, the momentum of both parts should be equal and opposite in direction.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>P</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></math></p><p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>P</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math> is momentum</p><p>De-Broglie wavelength is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mi>P</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence, both will have same de Broglie wavelength.</p><p>Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p></blockquote>",
